## Purpose

This PR turns AB's production-realization criterion into an executable fail-closed checker for finite declared grammars. It prevents an abstract terminal lower witness from being promoted to a production lower bound unless the representation, move lifting, registry, witness, objective, and interaction conditions are all present.

## Checker obligations

For a content-bound finite certificate it verifies:

- feasible registered production states;
- support-preserving representation;
- strict support descent;
- semantic preservation;
- objective nonincrease;
- exact production lifting of every weak move;
- the weak normalization ceiling and exact weak terminal complexity;
- a production preimage of a maximum weak terminal;
- complete-move irreducibility of that witness;
- a declared complete production registry with source digest and argument;
- production intrinsic support computed from all reachable terminals; and
- local-peak joinability when confluence is claimed.

It emits `PRODUCTION_EXACT_TRANSFER_PASS`, `PROOF_LANGUAGE_WASTE_CERTIFIED`, or typed rejection/nontransfer terminals. It explicitly states that it cannot discover moves omitted from an external implementation.

## Controls

Eight registered controls cover:

- valid exact transfer;
- genuine proof-language waste;
- semantic unsoundness;
- objective increase;
- support-map mismatch;
- an unlifted weak move;
- an incomplete registry; and
- a nonconfluent production interaction.

Support preservation is an authoritative hard gate in V2.

## Codex cooperation request

Codex should encode the intended Pauli/TARE production grammar as a finite certificate on the smallest complete domains, bind the actual move-registry source digest, and run the checker. It must then independently review whether the finite registry is extensionally complete for the production implementation. A `PROOF_LANGUAGE_WASTE_CERTIFIED` terminal is not automatically a quantum compiler result; a missing move, unsound fusion, objective increase, or nonconfluent interaction remains blocking.

## Authority boundary

This PR validates the checker and finite declared certificates only. It does not establish completeness of an external compiler registry, a faithful Pauli/TARE map, production search value, or journal authority.

Clean child of PR #1423, which now owns the independently converged all-finite-abelian-group fusion theorem.

This PR adds only the genuinely new interaction layer:

- after component terminal witnesses are realized, a verified pairwise reducing cross-move registry induces a terminal interaction graph H;
- exact global terminal certificate budget is `alpha(H)`;
- loss of naive additive product budget is `|V|-alpha(H)=tau(H)`;
- weighted version is maximum-weight independent set / complementary weighted vertex cover;
- exact global certificate computation can therefore become NP-hard purely from cross-component interactions, despite trivial local components;
- forest/bipartite/bounded-treewidth/disconnected cases inherit classical tractable algorithms;
- minimal higher-order cross moves induce a hypergraph independence/transversal version.

Classical graph theory is donor-owned. The residual is the exact move-registry-to-certificate mapping and its use as a quantitative production audit.

Finite verifier exhausts all 1,099 simple graphs through five vertices and compares simulated terminal states with alpha/tau. Production transfer remains open under #1385.

